# Line endings — CI runs on Linux, so executable text must be checked out LF.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Every shell script in this repository is executed by a Linux
# runner (ci.yml) or inside a Linux container (the qe-explorer / qe-central
# images, scripts/qec_ci_db_setup.sh, ci/run_*_tests.sh). Bash cannot parse a
# CRLF script: it reads the carriage return as part of the last token and fails
# with a syntax error that names a line which is visibly correct —
#
#     scripts/gate_rollback.sh: line 33: syntax error near unexpected token $'in\r'
#     scripts/gate_rollback.sh: line 33: `  case "$1" in'
#
# — which is exactly as confusing to debug as it looks. Observed on this repo:
# tests/deploy_gate reported "12 failed, 11 errors" against a CRLF working tree
# and "116 passed" against the identical files normalised to LF. Nothing was
# wrong with the code.
#
# Without this file the outcome depends on each developer's `core.autocrlf`.
# That happens to be `true` on the current Windows checkout, so its commits are
# normalised to LF and CI is fine today. A contributor with `core.autocrlf=false`
# would commit CRLF and break every shell-script job on Linux — a repository-wide
# breakage caused by a local git setting nobody looks at.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the file types where the line ending is FUNCTIONAL
# rather than cosmetic, instead of a blanket `* text=auto` that would rewrite
# every file in the tree as a side effect of a CI fix.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf

# Interpreter shebang lines have the same problem as bash: a `#!/usr/bin/env
# python3\r` is not a valid interpreter path. Python source is also read by
# tools (ruff, pytest assertion rewriting) that report CR as content.
*.py      text eol=lf

# Consumed by Linux tooling: docker build, alembic, helm, kubectl, GitHub Actions.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf

# PowerShell is run by Windows hosts (scripts/deploy.ps1 on the operator's box),
# so it keeps CRLF rather than being dragged into the Linux convention.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Recorded evidence — goldens, characterization manifests and crawl recordings.
#
# WHY. These files are compared BYTE FOR BYTE, both by pytest (the M0.3
# characterization gate) and by CI itself (`git diff --quiet -- tests/browser/golden`
# and `-- evidence/m23_retirement`). The recorder always writes an LF newline.
# Without a rule here, `core.autocrlf=true` — which is the setting on the current
# Windows checkout — checks them out with CRLF, and the very next re-record
# rewrites every line back to LF. `git status` then reports the whole file dirty
# with no behaviour change behind it, and the CI "a golden was rewritten" guard
# cannot tell that apart from a real capture regression.
#
# Pinning them to LF makes a clean clone byte-identical on Linux and Windows, so
# a diff in one of these files always means the CAPTURE changed.
*.golden  text eol=lf

# …AND THE SAME ARTEFACTS THAT ARE SHAPED LIKE JSON, which the rule above does
# not reach. The section says "characterization manifests and crawl recordings";
# `*.golden` was the only pattern pinned, so every JSON-shaped recording was left
# `unspecified` and therefore CRLF in a Windows working copy and LF in the blob.
#
# MEASURED, on evidence/a23_live_network/manifest.jsonl: 20 CRLF in the working
# copy, 0 in the committed blob. Nothing broke — the guards that read these files
# use `Path.read_text()`, which applies universal newlines and normalises CRLF
# away before hashing — but that is invisible in the code and one refactor deep:
#
#     read_text()  -> 1f4f4f7d…  matches the sha256 pinned in stamp.json
#     read_bytes() -> fe50c5ac…  DOES NOT
#
# `read_bytes()` is the obvious "hash the actual file" change for someone to make,
# and it would fail on Windows only, reporting the evidence as EDITED when it is
# byte-identical to what CI recorded. Pinning eol=lf removes the trap instead of
# relying on a reader noticing it.
#
# The browser goldens are here for the same reason and are not hypothetical: CI
# guards them with `git diff --quiet -- tests/browser/golden`, which is exactly
# the comparison a line-ending difference corrupts.
Nexus_power/evidence/**                                   text eol=lf
Nexus_power/engines/qe-explorer/tests/browser/golden/**    text eol=lf

# AND PINNING IS NOT RETROACTIVE. The attribute applies at checkout and at
# `git add --renormalize`; a file already sitting on disk keeps its CRLF until
# one of those happens. Measured immediately after adding the rule above: one
# evidence file read 0 CRLF (it had been re-checked-out to verify the fix) while
# four others still read 218, 187, 2360 and 13. "Pinned" and "actually LF on
# disk" are different states, and only the first survives a clean clone.
#
# So a checkout that predates this rule still carries the trap locally even
# though CI does not. `git rm --cached -r <path> && git checkout -- <path>`, or
# simply deleting and re-checking-out the files, closes it — but check
# `git status` first: re-checkout destroys uncommitted work, and this tree is
# shared between sessions.

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tgz     binary
*.zip     binary
